<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Lab 3, Due: October 23, 2026</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            <term>Instructions:</term> Complete the following exercises in your
            groups.
            For the write up:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        You should type up your report (a google docs version is
                        available on Blackboard).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Include screenshots of your work in Sage.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        You only need to hand in one report for your group.
                        Only include the names of students who contributed.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        This lab is graded out of 30 points.
                        Each problem will be graded out of 5 points - you earn 5
                        points by including your reasoning, following all
                        directions, and making only minor errors.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

        <p>
            There is a page of Sage cells available
            <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/0Ng" visual="http://gvsu.edu/s/0Ng">here</url>.
        </p>

        <p>
            In this lab, we'll see how computer animators use matrix
            transformations to tell stores.
            In fact, when you watch a Pixar movie, you're actually seeing some
            very elegant applications of linear algebra.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now, let me introduce you to Wooody, the lovable star of the
            animated kids' film <em>Toy Tale</em> due out this holiday season.
            <image source="images/lab3_wooody1.png" width="35%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Introduction to Wooody from <em>Toy Tale</em>.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </p>

        <p>
            The computer animators who made <em>Toy Tale</em> assembled the film
            frame by frame.
            To make use think that Wooody is moving, they make changes in
            Wooody's position from one frame to the next.
            This is where linear algebra is useful.
            (Actually, Wooody really lives in a three-dimensional world and each
            part of his body may be moving in different ways so the situation is
            a little more complicated, but hopefully you will get the idea.)
        </p>

        <p>
            If you go to
            <url href="http://gvsu.edu/s/0Jb" visual="http://gvsu.edu/s/0Jb">this website</url>,
            you will find a figure that can make Wooody move.
            On the left, you see a picture of Wooody, while on the right you
            will see a picture of him after a matrix transformation has been
            applied.
        </p>

        <p>
            Here is the function we will consider; it is not quite as simple as
            what we have seen in class:
            <md>
                <mrow>
                                x_r \amp = ax_1 + by_1 + c 
                                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                                y_r \amp = dx_1 + ey_1 + f 
                                </mrow>
            </md>
            The six sliders along the top of the diagram represent the
            quantities
            <md>
                <mrow>
                                 a \,\,\, b \,\,\, c 
                                </mrow>
                <mrow>
                                 d \,\,\, e \,\,\, f
                                </mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Using homogeneous coordinates, we represent this change as
                <md>
                    \threevec{x_r}{y_r}{1} = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \amp c \\
        d \amp e \amp f \\
        0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
        \end{bmatrix} \threevec{x_1}{y_1}{1}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute this matrix product and verify that it produces the
                    appropriate expressions for <m>x_r</m> and <m>y_r</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The benefit of using homogeneous coordinates is because they
                    introduce additional parameters <m>c</m> and <m>f</m>.
                    What role do these two parameters play in the
                    transformation?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Press the <term>Reset</term> button if you have modified Wooody.
                As shown above, Wooody is waving with his left hand.
                In the first scene of the movie, Wooody comes on screen and
                waves with his right hand.
                What is the matrix transformation that has been applied?
            </p>

            <image source="images/lab3_wooody2.png" width="35%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Wooody waving his right hand.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Next, Wooody begins his morning calisthenics and performs a
                cartwheel in the following frames reading across the first row
                and then the bottom.
            </p>

            <image source="images/lab3_wooody3.png" width="50%">
                <shortdescription>Wooody doing a cartwheel.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the transformation <m>M_1</m> that produces the
                    first (upper left) frame?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Using <m>M_1</m>, how can you produce the second frame
                    (upper right)?
                    What is the matrix <m>M_2</m> that produces the second
                    frame?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How can you continue using <m>M_1</m> to generate <m>M_3</m>
                    and <m>M_4</m>, the matrices that produce the remaining two
                    frames?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                At this point in the story, Wooody remembers a long-ago love and
                the camera moves in for a close up.
            </p>

            <image source="images/lab3_wooody4.png" width="60%">
                <shortdescription>
                    Wooody in love and close up.
                </shortdescription>
            </image>

            <p>
                There are two ways to do this.
                The first two frames are similar to previous questions.
                The third is a little tricky but the diagram gives you another
                tool to help.
                Once you have the diagram configured in some way you life, you
                may press the button <term>Apply</term>.
                The image on the left is set to that on the right and the
                transformation is reset to the identity.
                You may then operate on the new figure from scratch.
                (Mathematically, you are composing two
                functions/transformations.)
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain a way to make the sequence of frames above.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the function that takes our original picture of
                    Wooody into the final close up.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Wooody decides to go out for a walk.
                In the morning sun, he casts a shadow that looks like this:
            </p>

            <image source="images/lab3_wooody5.png" width="35%">
                <shortdescription>Wooody's shadow on a walk.</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the matrix transformation that creates the shadow.
                    You might remember how we have found the matrix representing
                    a matrix transformation by looking at what happens to the
                    vectors <m>\evec_1</m> and <m>\evec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    As the sun comes up, his shadow gets shorter.
                </p>

                <image source="images/lab3_wooody6.png" width="50%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Wooody's shadow gets shorter.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    Explain what transformations achieve this and how you found
                    them.
                    (Think about composing transformations again.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>